A 58-year-old man presents to the emergency department with sudden onset crushing chest pain radiating to his left arm for the past 30 minutes. He is diaphoretic and anxious. His BP is 150/90 mmHg, HR 100 bpm. ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF.

Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?

AOral metoprololBAspirin 325 mg + activate catheterization labCThrombolytic therapyDEchocardiography

Check AnswerSkip to profile setup

✓Aspirin 325 mg + activate catheterization labCThrombolytic therapyDEchocardiography

1/1 Correct!

This patient is presenting with an acute inferior STEMI. The initial management includes aspirin for antiplatelet therapy and emergent activation of the catheterization lab for primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI), which is the gold standard for STEMI management when available within 90 minutes.

