Why this is correct

Copy explanation

APre-oxygenate the patient with 100% oxygen for 30–60 seconds before inserting the suction catheter

Pre-oxygenation with 100% oxygen for 30–60 seconds before suctioning is the correct first step and is recommended by current evidence-based guidelines for tracheostomy care. This patient has a baseline SpO2 of 91%, which is already below the normal range. Suctioning is known to cause transient hypoxemia by removing oxygen along with secretions. Pre-oxygenation creates a reservoir of oxygen in the lungs to buffer this expected desaturation and reduces the risk of dysrhythmias, agitation, and respiratory distress during the procedure. The nurse should also limit each suction pass to no more than 10–15 seconds and allow recovery time between passes.

Option B is incorrect because applying continuous suction while withdrawing causes greater mucosal trauma and removes more oxygen than intermittent suction technique. Current guidelines recommend applying suction only during withdrawal using intermittent or rotating technique, and the catheter should be inserted only to the pre-measured length or just beyond the tip of the tracheostomy tube—not advanced to the carina, which increases the risk of mucosal injury, coughing, bronchospasm, and vagal stimulation.

Option C is incorrect because suction pressure exceeding 150 mmHg (adult range: 80–150 mmHg) is not recommended. Pressures of 200 mmHg significantly increase the risk of airway mucosal damage, hemorrhage, and atelectasis. For adults with thick secretions, the appropriate response is to use the upper limit of the recommended range (up to 150 mmHg) and consider saline instillation only if explicitly indicated, though this practice is not routinely recommended.

Option D is incorrect because placing the patient supine and flat does not facilitate tracheostomy suctioning and may actually worsen respiratory mechanics by reducing functional residual capacity, particularly in a patient with COPD and existing hypoxemia. The recommended position for tracheostomy suctioning is semi-Fowler's position (30–45 degrees head elevation) to improve lung expansion and reduce aspiration risk.

